  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nWhile cooler air has invaded our region today behind last night's \ncold frontal passage, an even stronger push of colder air will \narrive over the coming weekend. Skies should begin to clear out \nlater this afternoon through the evening at least across the western \nhalf of our area. Along with winds becoming light, this may set the \nstage for some fog development early Thursday morning, particularly \ngiven the heavier rains we just received. While fog is most likely \nfrom the MO bootheel region into far west KY and perhaps extreme \nsouthern IL, the signal is even stronger just to our south down in \nportions of AR/TN.\n\nA positively tilted trough will swing through Thursday night into \nFriday morning pushing another cold front across our region. \nBasically all guidance shows a dry passage at this point, but \nwouldn't be surprised if we squeeze out a few sprinkles or very \nlight showers. Northwest winds usher in the coolest airmass of the \nFall season so far with highs likely stuck in the 50s along and \nnorth of the Ohio River and back into much of southeast MO on \nFriday. Saturday looks even chillier with widespread mid 50s across \nthe region. This likely will be the coldest daytime high temperature \nin Paducah since late March. There is also a concern of many areas \nobserving their first freeze of the season Saturday night. NBM is \ncertainly more aggressive with the chances though, highlighting a 50-\n80% chance at lows falling to 32 for most of the region. Meanwhile \nthe LREF grand ensemble probability of hitting 32 is less than 20%. \n\nDry conditions are likely to continue through at least mid to late \nnext week. Southerly flow returns by Monday allowing highs to push \nback into the 60s. While there may be another frontal passage \nsometime mid to late next week, the precipitation prospects appear \nbleak with ensembles suggesting less than a tenth of an inch at this \ntime.",
